ON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n2990,JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION,\"**Entity: JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION**\n\nThe Journal of the American Statistical Association (JASA) is a leading academic journal that serves as a platform for the dissemination of research in the field of statistics. It is particularly noted for publishing significant contributions to statistical methodologies, applications, and theoretical advancements. Among its notable publications is the work of C. W. Dunnett, which focuses on multiple comparison procedures, a critical area in the analysis of variance and experimental design.\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nJASA aims to advance the field of statistics by publishing high-quality research articles that cover a wide range of statistical topics, including but not limited to experimental design, statistical theory, and applied statistics. The journal serves as a resource for statisticians, researchers, and practitioners seeking to stay informed about the latest developments and methodologies in the discipline.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nWhile JASA itself does not have a specific mathematical formulation, the articles published within it often include various statistical models and formulations relevant to the research being presented. For instance, studies on multiple comparison procedures may involve models that account for Type I error rates and power analysis in the context of hypothesis testing.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe journal does not have a specific experimental design layout, as it publishes a variety of research articles that may employ different experimental designs, including completely randomized designs, factorial designs, and more complex designs such as mixed models or hierarchical models.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of the research published in JASA depends on the adherence to the assumptions of the statistical methods employed. Common assumptions include normality of residuals, homogeneity of variances, independence of observations, and appropriate sample size considerations.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nResearchers and practitioners should consider submitting their work to JASA when their research contributes new insights or methodologies to the field of statistics. The journal is particularly suitable for studies that advance theoretical understanding or provide practical applications of statistical methods.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedures presented in JASA articles vary widely depending on the specific research question and methodology. Common procedures may include hypothesis testing, regression analysis, and various forms of ANOVA, each with its own set of assumptions and analytical techniques.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nJASA is recognized for its rigorous peer-review process, which ensures the quality and credibility of the research it publishes. The journal also has a broad readership, making it an influential platform for disseminating statistical knowledge.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nOne limitation of JASA is that, due to its focus on high-quality research, the acceptance rate for submissions can be low, which may discourage some researchers. Additionally, the complexity of some articles may limit accessibility for practitioners who are not deeply versed in advanced statistical theory.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nJASA publishes research related to various statistical methodologies, including but not limited to Bayesian statistics, machine learning, and non-parametric methods. Alternative journals may include the Annals of Statistics or the Journal of Statistical Software, which also focus on statistical methodologies and applications.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers should ensure that their studies are well-designed and that they adhere to the statistical assumptions relevant to their chosen methodologies. Clear communication of results and implications is essential for publication in JASA.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nWhile JASA itself does not provide software, many articles published within it may reference statistical software packages such as R, SAS, or SPSS for the implementation of the methodologies discussed.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCommon applications of the research published in JASA include clinical trials, agricultural experiments, quality control studies, and social science research, where statistical methods are essential for data analysis and interpretation. The journal serves as a vital resource for statisticians working across various fields.\",19\r\n3276,BIBLIOGRAPHY,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Bibliography\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nA bibliography is a systematic list of sources and references that have been cited or consulted in the preparation of academic and professional works. Its primary purpose is to provide credit to the original authors of the works referenced, facilitate further research by readers, and demonstrate the depth of research conducted by the author.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nWhile a bibliography does not have a mathematical formulation or model structure in the traditional sense, it can be organized according to specific citation styles (e.g., APA, MLA, Chicago) that dictate the format of each entry, including author names, publication year, title, and source.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nThe structure of a bibliography typically includes:\n- Author(s) of the work\n- Title of the work\n- Publication information (e.g., publisher, year of publication)\n- Format (e.g., book, journal article, website)\n- DOI or URL (if applicable)\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nFor a bibliography to be valid, it is assumed that:\n- All cited works are accurately represented and correctly formatted.\n- The sources are credible and relevant to the subject matter.\n- The author has adhered to the ethical standards of citation to avoid plagiarism.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nA bibliography is essential in any academic or professional writing that requires the citation of sources. It is particularly applicable in research papers, theses, dissertations, and articles where the author needs to substantiate claims or provide a foundation for their arguments. Selection criteria for sources may include relevance, credibility, and the date of publication.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nWhile there is no formal analysis procedure for a bibliography itself, the process of compiling a bibliography involves:\n1. Identifying relevant sources.\n2. Evaluating the credibility and relevance of each source.\n3. Formatting the entries according to the chosen citation style.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Enhances the credibility of the work by demonstrating thorough research.\n- Provides readers with resources for further study.\n- Helps to avoid plagiarism by properly attributing ideas to their original authors.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Compiling a comprehensive bibliography can be time-consuming.\n- The quality of the bibliography is dependent on the quality of the sources selected.\n- Inconsistent citation practices can lead to confusion or misinterpretation.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated to bibliographies are literature reviews, which synthesize and analyze the findings of multiple sources rather than simply listing them. Other alternatives include annotated bibliographies, which provide summaries and evaluations of each source.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen creating a bibliography, authors should consider:\n- The specific citation style required by their institution or publication.\n- The need for consistency in formatting throughout the document.\n- The importance of keeping track of sources during the research process to simplify the bibliography compilation.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nVarious software tools can assist in creating and managing bibliographies, such as reference management software like EndNote, Zotero, and Mendeley. These tools can automate citation formatting and help organize sources efficiently.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nBibliographies are commonly found in academic papers, theses, dissertations, and scholarly articles across various fields, including the sciences, humanities, and social sciences. They serve as a critical component in any research endeavor, ensuring that authors acknowledge the contributions of others in their work.\",1\r\n3310,\"ANDERSON, V. L.\",,1\r\n3296,\"BOX, G. E. P.\",A prominent statistician known for his work in experimental design and quality control.,1\r\n3297,\"COCHRAN, W. G.\",A statistician known for his work in experimental design and analysis of variance.,1\r\n3298,\"CARMER, S. G.\",A researcher who has contributed to statistical methodologies and multiple comparison procedures.,1\r\n3299,\"CHALONER, K.\",A statistician known for work in Bayesian designs and statistical planning.,1\r\n3301,D. E. COLEMAN,A statistician known for contributions to industrial experimentation and quality control.,1\r\n3302,D. R. COX,A statistician known for his work in design of experiments and statistical theory.,1\r\n3303,D. R. SITTER,A researcher known for contributions to statistical analysis and experimental design.,1\r\n3304,N. R. DRAPER,A statistician known for work in regression analysis and experimental design.,1\r\n3305,N. L. FRANKLIN,A researcher known for contributions to statistical analysis and quality technology.,1\r\n3306,A. H. BOOTH,A statistician known for work in statistical methods and experimental design.,1\r\n3307,G. J. LIEBERMAN,A researcher known for contributions to engineering statistics.,1\r\n3308,M. R. SWANSON,A statistician known for work in statistical methodologies and quality control.,1\r\n3309,K. H. BOOTH,A researcher known for contributions to statistical design and analysis.,1\r\n2976,BIOSTATISTICS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Biostatistics**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**:  \nBiostatistics is a specialized field that applies statistical methods to biological, health-related, and medical research. Its primary purpose is to analyze and interpret data derived from biological experiments and health studies, facilitating informed decision-making in public health, clinical trials, epidemiology, and other areas of health sciences.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**:  \nBiostatistics encompasses a variety of statistical models and methods, including but not limited to regression analysis, survival analysis, and hypothesis testing. The specific mathematical formulations depend on the type of data and research questions being addressed. For instance, linear regression can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_nX_n + \\epsilon \\]\n\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( X_1, X_2, ..., X_n \\) are predictor variables, \\( \\beta_0, \\beta_1, ..., \\beta_n \\) are coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**:  \nIn biostatistical studies, experimental designs may include randomized controlled trials, cohort studies, case-control studies, and cross-sectional studies. Each design has its own structure, focusing on how subjects are assigned to treatments or how data is collected to ensure valid and reliable results.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**:  \nThe validity of biostatistical analyses often relies on several assumptions, including:\n- Independence of observations\n- Normality of the data (for parametric tests)\n- Homogeneity of variance (for ANOVA)\n- Linearity (for regression models)\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**:  \nBiostatistics is applicable in various scenarios, including clinical trials, epidemiological studies, and public health research. Selection criteria for specific statistical methods depend on the research question, data type (continuous, categorical), sample size, and study design.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**:  \nThe analysis procedure in biostatistics typically involves:\n1. Data collection and preparation\n2. Exploratory data analysis to understand data distributions and relationships\n3. Selection of appropriate statistical methods based on the research question and data characteristics\n4. Application of statistical tests (e.g., t-tests, ANOVA, regression analysis)\n5. Interpretation of results and drawing conclusions\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**:  \nBiostatistics provides robust tools for analyzing complex biological data, allowing researchers to make evidence-based conclusions. It enhances the understanding of health-related phenomena and supports the development of effective interventions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**:  \nDespite its strengths, biostatistics has limitations, including potential biases in study design, challenges in data collection, and the complexity of statistical models that may lead to misinterpretation if not properly understood.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**:  \nBiostatistics is closely related to other fields such as epidemiology and bioinformatics. Alternative methods may include machine learning techniques for predictive modeling or qualitative research methods for exploratory studies.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**:  \nWhen implementing biostatistical methods, researchers must consider the quality of data, the appropriateness of the chosen statistical methods, and the need for software tools to perform complex analyses.\n\n**Software Implementation**:  \nCommon software tools used in biostatistics include R, SAS, SPSS, and Python, which provide extensive libraries and functions for statistical analysis and data visualization.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**:  \nBiostatistics is widely used in clinical trials to evaluate the efficacy of new drugs, in epidemiological studies to identify risk factors for diseases, and in public health research to assess health interventions and policies. Examples include analyzing the effectiveness of vaccines, studying the impact of lifestyle factors on health outcomes, and evaluating the prevalence of diseases in populations.\",3\r\n555,BIOMETRIKA,\"**Entity: BIOMETRIKA**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nBiometrika is a prominent academic journal that focuses on the publication of research related to statistical theory and methodology, particularly in the context of design of experiments and statistical analysis. It serves as a platform for disseminating significant advancements in statistical science, including experimental design, statistical tables, and various methodologies that underpin statistical analysis.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nWhile Biometrika itself does not have a specific mathematical formulation, it publishes articles that may include various statistical models and formulations relevant to experimental design and analysis of variance (ANOVA). These models often involve the use of statistical equations to describe relationships between variables, assess effects, and evaluate hypotheses.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nAs a journal, Biometrika does not have a design layout in the traditional sense of experimental designs. However, it features articles that may describe various experimental designs, including factorial designs, response surface methodologies, and other structured approaches to experimentation.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe articles published in Biometrika often rely on standard assumptions in statistical analysis, such as normality of residuals, homogeneity of variances, independence of observations, and the correct specification of models. These assumptions are critical for the validity of the statistical inferences drawn from the research.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nBiometrika is applicable to researchers and practitioners in the field of statistics who are interested in the latest developments in statistical theory and methodologies. It is particularly relevant for those involved in experimental design, data analysis, and the application of statistical methods in various disciplines.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe journal publishes articles that may outline various analysis procedures, including ANOVA, regression analysis, and other statistical tests. These procedures are essential for interpreting experimental data and drawing conclusions based on statistical evidence.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nBiometrika is recognized for its rigorous peer-review process and its commitment to high-quality research. It provides a comprehensive resource for statisticians and researchers seeking to stay informed about the latest methodologies and theoretical advancements in the field.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nAs a specialized journal, Biometrika may not cover all aspects of applied statistics or experimental design, potentially limiting its audience to those with a strong background in statistical theory. Additionally, the complexity of some articles may pose challenges for practitioners who are less familiar with advanced statistical concepts.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nBiometrika is related to other statistical journals and publications that focus on experimental design and statistical analysis, such as the Journal of the American Statistical Association (JASA) and Technometrics. These journals also publish significant research in the field and may serve as alternatives for researchers.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers looking to publish in Biometrika should ensure that their work adheres to the journal's submission guidelines and standards for statistical rigor. It is also important to clearly articulate the relevance and implications of their findings within the broader context of statistical methodology.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nWhile Biometrika itself does not provide software, many of the statistical methods and designs discussed in the journal can be implemented using statistical software packages such as R, SAS, and SPSS. These tools are commonly used for conducting analyses described in the articles.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nBiometrika publishes research that spans a wide range of applications, including agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and social science studies. Examples of articles may include those that develop new statistical methods for analyzing experimental data or that provide insights into the design of effective experiments in various fields.\",4\r\n2880,FREEDOM,\"**Comprehensive Summary of \"\"Freedom\"\" in Statistical Context**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nIn the context of statistics, \"\"freedom\"\" primarily refers to the concept of degrees of freedom (df), which is a critical component in various statistical analyses, including hypothesis testing and the estimation of parameters. Degrees of freedom represent the number of independent values or quantities that can vary in an analysis without violating any constraints. This concept is essential for determining the distribution of test statistics and for making inferences about population parameters based on sample data.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nDegrees of freedom can be mathematically expressed in various contexts. For example, in a one-sample t-test, the degrees of freedom are calculated as \\( df = n - 1 \\), where \\( n \\) is the sample size. In the context of ANOVA (Analysis of Variance), the degrees of freedom are partitioned into between-group and within-group components, typically represented as:\n- Between-group df: \\( k - 1 \\) (where \\( k \\) is the number of groups)\n- Within-group df: \\( N - k \\) (where \\( N \\) is the total number of observations)\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of statistical tests that utilize degrees of freedom relies on several assumptions, including:\n1. Independence of observations: Each observation must be independent of others.\n2. Normality: The data should be approximately normally distributed, especially for small sample sizes.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variances among groups should be equal (for ANOVA).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nDegrees of freedom are applicable in various statistical tests, including t-tests, ANOVA, regression analysis, and chi-square tests. The selection of the appropriate test depends on the research question, the number of groups being compared, and the nature of the data (e.g., continuous vs. categorical).\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nIn statistical analysis, degrees of freedom are used to determine the critical values from statistical distributions (e.g., t-distribution, F-distribution) that are necessary for hypothesis testing. The calculated test statistic is compared against these critical values to assess the significance of the results.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe concept of degrees of freedom allows for more accurate statistical inference by accounting for the number of independent pieces of information available in the data. It enhances the robustness of statistical tests and helps in the proper interpretation of results.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nOne limitation of degrees of freedom is that they can be misunderstood or misapplied, leading to incorrect conclusions. Additionally, the assumptions underlying the use of degrees of freedom may not always hold true, particularly in small samples or when data are skewed.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nDegrees of freedom are integral to various statistical methods, including:\n- t-tests\n- ANOVA\n- Regression analysis\n- Chi-square tests\n\nAlternative methods, such as non-parametric tests, may be used when the assumptions of traditional tests are violated, although these alternatives may not always utilize degrees of freedom in the same manner.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing statistical tests that involve degrees of freedom, researchers should ensure that the assumptions are met and consider the sample size, as small samples can lead to unreliable estimates of degrees of freedom and, consequently, misleading results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMost statistical software packages (e.g., R, SPSS, SAS, Python's SciPy) automatically calculate degrees of freedom as part of their statistical analysis functions, providing users with the necessary outputs for hypothesis testing.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nDegrees of freedom are commonly used in various fields, including psychology, medicine, and social sciences, where researchers conduct experiments or observational studies to test hypotheses about population parameters. For instance, in clinical trials, degrees of freedom are crucial for analyzing the effectiveness of treatments compared to control groups.\",3\r\n556,PROFESSOR E. S. PEARSON,Professor E. S. Pearson is mentioned as a significant figure in the context of Biometrika and statistical design.,1\r\n2977,PEARSON AND HARTLEY,E. S. Pearson and H. O. Hartley are authors known for their contributions to statistical tables and methodologies.,1\r\n2989,DUNNETT,C. W. Dunnett is a statistician known for developing Dunnett's Test for comparing treatments with a control in statistical analysis.,2\r\n2975,ROTANIMONE,Rotanimone is an organization involved in the analysis of statistical models and variance in research.,1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n4367,JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION,A. A. ALMIMI,A. A. Almimi has published research in the Journal of the American Statistical Association,23\r\n4369,JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION,BIBLIOGRAPHY,The bibliography includes references to articles published in the Journal of the American Statistical Association,20\r\n4370,JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION,\"ANDERSON, V. L.\",\"Anderson, V. L. has published research in the Journal of the American Statistical Association\",20\r\n4371,JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION,\"BOX, G. E. P.\",\"Box, G. E. P. has published research in the Journal of the American Statistical Association\",20\r\n4372,JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION,\"COCHRAN, W. G.\",\"Cochran, W. G. has published research in the Journal of the American Statistical Association\",20\r\n4373,JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION,\"CARMER, S. G.\",\"Carmer, S. G. has published research in the Journal of the American Statistical Association\",20\r\n4374,JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN STATISTICAL ASSOCIATION,\"CHALONER, K.\",\"Chaloner, K. has p